Parent Goal: #14
Source audit: #125
Feeds: #136, #27, #29, #105, #111
Baseline finding: `15c4e0b1cfb222087800dabeab90fb17cb86bd63`

## Problem
Audit #125 found the PostgreSQL connection default is `SslMode::Disable`. For a non-local PostgreSQL host this permits credentials and query/data traffic to be sent without TLS unless the user explicitly changes the mode.

Audit #125 classified this as P1. RC1 severity rules do not allow a P1 connection-security gap to be accepted only through documentation.

## Required product decision
Implement one explicit secure-by-default policy for v0.1. The implementation may choose the smallest compatible design, but it must satisfy all of these:
- a newly configured non-local PostgreSQL connection must not silently default to plaintext transport;
- localhost/local-development workflows remain possible through an explicit user choice;
- the UI clearly communicates when TLS verification is weaker than `verify-full`;
- persisted/parsed connection settings round-trip deterministically;
- SQLite does not surface irrelevant TLS controls;
- unsupported custom CA/client-cert behavior remains accurately documented.

Possible implementations include a safer default plus explicit opt-out, or a host-aware UI policy. Do not silently invent `sslmode` semantics unsupported by the current domain/SQLx mapping.

## Tests / evidence
- default/new remote connection behavior test;
- localhost explicit-disable path test;
- domain -> SQLx mode mapping tests remain green;
- persisted round-trip tests;
- UI warning/selection tests for insecure mode;
- exact-head frontend/Rust relevant gates before completion.

## Merge gate
P1 remains open until the secure-default policy is accepted on one exact SHA and #125/#136 are updated.

